tion", "lp": -1.2523562908172607, "top": [[" documentation", -1.2523562908172607], [" (`", -1.2995498180389404]]}, {"t": " (`", "lp": -1.1155742406845093, "top": [[" (`", -1.1155742406845093], [" at", -1.580969214439392]]}, {"t": "Documentation", "lp": -0.09323546290397644, "top": [["Documentation", -0.09323546290397644], ["drivers", -2.6463894844055176]]}, {"t": "/admin", "lp": -0.6658718585968018, "top": [["/admin", -0.6658718585968018], ["/g", -1.327698826789856]]}, {"t": "-guide", "lp": -0.018120337277650833, "top": [["-guide", -0.018120337277650833], ["-guid", -4.152441501617432]]}, {"t": "/", "lp": -0.16118457913398743, "top": [["/", -0.16118457913398743], ["/kernel", -2.009166955947876]]}, {"t": "...", "lp": -0.44631823897361755, "top": [["...", -0.44631823897361755], ["\u2026", -1.9898878335952759]]}, {"t": "`", "lp": -0.3166962265968323, "top": [["`", -0.3166962265968323], ["`)", -1.3116207122802734]]}, {"t": " or", "lp": -0.5154593586921692, "top": [[" or", -0.5154593586921692], [" for", -1.1441730260849]]}, {"t": " the", "lp": -0.09272805601358414, "top": [[" the", -0.09272805601358414], [" driver", -3.274048328399658]]}, {"t": " Xe", "lp": -0.6192833781242371, "top": [[" Xe", -0.6192833781242371], [" xe", -1.4440361261367798]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Slice (CCS) contexts are handled by the Xe driver. Here's what each mode does:\n\n## CCS Mode 1\n- **Context stealing / preemption-based**\n- CCS contexts are allocated dynamically from the pool of available compute slices\n- The driver can \"steal\" compute slices from other contexts when needed\n- More flexible but can introduce **preemption overhead** and potential latency spikes\n\n## CCS Mode 2\n- **Static/dedicated allocation**\n- Compute slices are assigned more statically to contexts\n- Generally provides **more predictable performance** since there's less dynamic rebalancing\n- Lower overhead from preemption\n\n## CCS Mode 4\n- This is typically the **most restrictive/dedicated** mode\n- Compute slices are **strictly partitioned** with minimal sharing\n- Used when you need **deterministic behavior** and want to avoid any interference between contexts\n\n## When to Use Each\n\n| Mode | Best for |\n|------|----------|\n| **1** | General use, maximum flexibility, mixed workloads |\n| **2** | Balanced performance, workloads wanting predictability |\n| **4** | Latency-sensitive or real-time workloads, benchmarking, scenarios where you need guaranteed isolation between compute contexts |\n\n---\n\n**A note of caution:** I want to be transparent that my knowledge of the exact semantics of these specific `ccs_mode` values in the Xe driver may not be fully precise. The parameter is exposed through the `xe` kernel driver module options.\n\n**For accurate, version-specific guidance, I'd recommend:**\n- Checking `modinfo xe` on your system for the documented options\n- Reviewing the kernel source documentation (`Documentation/admin-guide/...` or the Xe"}